---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const title = "Start here";
const description = "Try a small AI context workflow: capture a decision, ask a bounded question, check the answer and keep a reusable handoff.";
const sample = `# Repository handoff\n\nGoal: document how this repository starts locally.\nDecision: use the commands in README.md as the starting point.\nEvidence: README.md, section “Development”.\nUncertainty: the commands have not been run in this environment.\nNext step: run the documented command and record its result.\nConstraint: do not change application code for this check.`;
const prompt = `Read this handoff and the Development section of README.md.\nList the exact prerequisites and startup command.\nCite the file and heading for each answer.\nIf the README does not answer something, say what is missing.\nDo not infer that the command works just because it is documented.\nDo not change files or run commands yet.`;
---
<Base title={title} description={description} route="/start-here/">
  <p class="eyebrow">A first workflow · no installation required</p>
  <p class="lede">Turn one piece of work into context you can check and reuse.</p>
  <p>When a new agent session starts, a long transcript can obscure the decision that matters. A small handoff gives the next session a goal, the source behind it and an explicit next step. Try this with one repository you already know.</p>

  <h2>1. Capture the decision and its limits</h2>
  <p>Write a short Markdown file. Use this example as a template and replace its paths and headings with ones that exist in your repository. The example describes a documentation check; it is not a claim that any command has passed.</p>
  <pre><code>{sample}</code></pre>
  <p>The uncertainty line matters. Without it, a later session may read “use this command” as “this command has been tested.” Keep the evidence and the action separate.</p>

  <h2>2. Ask one bounded question</h2>
  <p>Give your agent the handoff and the named source file. In a chat without file access, paste only the relevant section with its filename and heading.</p>
  <pre><code>{prompt}</code></pre>
  <p>A useful answer names the prerequisites, identifies the exact command, and points back to the section you supplied. If a prerequisite is missing, the answer should preserve that gap.</p>

  <h2>3. Check the result against the source</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open each cited heading. Confirm that it exists.</li>
    <li>Compare the command and prerequisites with the actual text.</li>
    <li>Separate what the source says from what the agent inferred.</li>
    <li>If you choose to run the command, record the environment and result. A command printed in an answer is not a successful test.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If the answer adds an unsupported prerequisite or invents a heading, correct the handoff before reusing it. More context does not automatically repair a bad source.</p>

  <h2>4. Keep only what the next session needs</h2>
  <p>Update the handoff with the observed result and the remaining step. Keep the source pointer. Move the detailed log to a separate file if it grows large. This gives the next session a concise continuation point and leaves the evidence available for inspection.</p>
  <p>For the design behind this separation, read <a href="/blog/state-that-survives-the-session/">State That Survives the Session</a>. It describes an architecture and its limits; it does not report a measured improvement in recall.</p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <ul class="resource-links">
    <li><a href="/skills/notes-to-llms-txt/">Organize a larger pile of notes</a><span>Read the notes-to-llms skill and its full prompt before installing it.</span></li>
    <li><a href="/blog/what-docs-llms-files-and-indexes-actually-save/">Decide whether more context is worth the cost</a><span>Inspect the 256-run experiment, raw results and generalization limits.</span></li>
    <li><a href="/tree/">Find reference material for a topic</a><span>Browse a published reference, follow its sources and download only what you need.</span></li>
    <li><a href="/downloads/">Choose a workbench</a><span>Use LLMSX for research, Skills Explorer for installed skills, or GitHub Explorer for repository work.</span></li>
  </ul>
</Base>
